@* SubmitSaleConfirmationDialog.razor — second-step confirmation modal for
   cart submission (the Submit Order button on the shop mini-cart, the
   full Cart page, and the mobile cart).

   CONTRACT (mirrors CancelConfirmationDialog.razor exactly):
     - Same 3-second countdown UX: the "Yes, submit my order" button is
       disabled for 3 seconds after the dialog opens, then becomes
       clickable. Submitting an order is irreversible FROM THE SHOPPER'S
       SIDE (a submitted order can only be cancelled by staff / support),
       so the countdown provides friction against reflex confirmations —
       the same reason sale cancellation uses it.
     - Same layout: warning header, body text summarizing the cart
       (item count + grand total, pre-formatted by the CALLER so each
       page keeps its own price formatter/culture), the irreversibility
       warning ("cannot be undone by you — to cancel, contact support"),
       and a two-button footer (No go back / Yes I'm sure).
     - Same DialogService.OpenAsync<SubmitSaleConfirmationDialog>
       contract — the parent opens this with LineCount + TotalDisplay
       parameters and receives a bool result (true = confirmed,
       false/null = aborted).

   DIFFERENCES FROM CancelConfirmationDialog:
     - The strong-highlighted context is the cart summary (item count +
       total) instead of a sale number. The total arrives ALREADY
       FORMATTED (each caller renders money through its own
       page formatter); only the item count is digit-localized here via
       CultureFormat.FormatDigits.
     - The body explicitly names the escape hatch the owner requires the
       shopper to see BEFORE submitting: once submitted, the order can no
       longer be changed or cancelled by the shopper — cancellation
       requires contacting support.

   LIFECYCLE & ROBUSTNESS (copied verbatim from CancelConfirmationDialog —
   the countdown loop, the CTS, and both catch guards are the hardened
   pattern the other countdown dialogs share):
     - OnInitializedAsync starts a 3-iteration countdown loop with 1s sleep
       between iterations. Each iteration updates `countdown` and calls
       StateHasChanged so the button text re-renders.
     - A CancellationTokenSource is created up-front; if the user closes
       the dialog before the countdown finishes (e.g. clicks "No, go back"),
       the Dispose() method cancels the CTS so the Task.Delay doesn't keep
       ticking in the background.
     - We catch BOTH OperationCanceledException (raised when the CTS is
       cancelled) AND ObjectDisposedException (raised if Dispose runs so
       fast that the next Task.Delay tries to access a disposed CTS).
     - The dialog is single-purpose — no Command dispatch here. The parent
       decides what to do based on the bool return value.
*@

@inject Radzen.DialogService DialogService
@inject IStringLocalizer<SubmitSaleConfirmationDialog> Loc
@implements IDisposable

<div class="submit-sale-confirmation-dialog" style="padding: 1rem;">
    <RadzenStack Gap="1rem">
        <RadzenText TextStyle="TextStyle.H6" Class="submit-sale-confirmation-dialog__warning">
            @Loc["WarningHeading"]
        </RadzenText>

        <RadzenText>
            @Loc["BodyIntro"]
            <strong>@string.Format(System.Globalization.CultureInfo.CurrentCulture, Loc["SummaryFormat"], CultureFormat.FormatDigits(LineCount.ToString(System.Globalization.CultureInfo.InvariantCulture)), TotalDisplay)</strong>.
            @Loc["BodyPermanent"]
            @Loc["BodySupport"]
        </RadzenText>

        <RadzenText>@Loc["BodyConfirm"]</RadzenText>

        <RadzenStack Orientation="Orientation.Horizontal" Gap="0.5rem" JustifyContent="JustifyContent.End">
            <RadzenButton Text="@Loc["ButtonNo"]"
                          ButtonStyle="ButtonStyle.Secondary"
                          Click="() => DialogService.Close(false)" />
            <RadzenButton Text="@ButtonText"
                          ButtonStyle="ButtonStyle.Danger"
                          Disabled="@(countdown > 0)"
                          Click="() => DialogService.Close(true)" />
        </RadzenStack>
    </RadzenStack>
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// Number of line items in the cart being submitted. Displayed
    /// digit-localized (Persian digits in fa-IR) inside the summary.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public int LineCount { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The cart's grand total, ALREADY FORMATTED by the caller (each page
    /// renders money through its own formatter — the desktop shop's
    /// FormatPrice, the mobile pages' m-* helpers). Passed as a string so
    /// the dialog never needs to know a page's money-formatting rules.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public string? TotalDisplay { get; set; }

    private int countdown = 3;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    // Button text shows the countdown while it's ticking, then becomes the
    // plain "Yes, submit my order" once the button is enabled. The format
    // string is localized so fa-IR can render the digits in Persian if it
    // wants to.
    private string ButtonText =>
        countdown > 0
            ? string.Format(System.Globalization.CultureInfo.CurrentCulture, Loc["ButtonYesCounting"], countdown)
            : Loc["ButtonYesReady"];

    protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        try
        {
            for (int i = 3; i > 0; i--)
            {
                countdown = i;
                await InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);
                // Capture the token locally so that even if Dispose races
                // ahead and nulls out _cts, we still have a valid reference
                // to pass to Task.Delay. (Dispose also calls Cancel first,
                // so a disposed-token race here surfaces as
                // OperationCanceledException, which we catch below.)
                var token = _cts.Token;
                await Task.Delay(1000, token);
            }
            countdown = 0;
            await InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) { /* user closed dialog before countdown finished */ }
        catch (ObjectDisposedException) { /* Dispose ran between iterations — same story */ }
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        try
        {
            _cts?.Cancel();
        }
        catch (ObjectDisposedException) { /* already disposed — fine */ }
        _cts?.Dispose();
        _cts = null;
    }
}
